| Project                | Expected outcome                            |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Disk investigation     | Produce a verified forensic image           |
| File recovery          | Recover test files from a prepared image    |
| Memory investigation   | Identify processes in a RAM capture         |
| Network investigation  | Reconstruct a controlled network event      |
| Mobile investigation   | Examine an authorised Android backup        |
| Email investigation    | Identify suspicious email indicators        |
| Malware investigation  | Analyse a harmless training sample          |
| Image investigation    | Inspect EXIF and image authenticity         |
| Database investigation | Identify changes in a test database         |
| Cloud investigation    | Reconstruct activity from sample audit logs |

Always work with devices, accounts, data and laboratory images you own or have explicit authority to investigate. Preserve originals and document every action.

### PART 7 – Recommended professional learning order

You do not need to master all 30 specialisations simultaneously. Build a broad foundation, then specialise.

### Your forensic learning roadmap

* Foundation level

    Computer architecture, operating systems, binary, file systems, networking and Python.

* Core forensic level

    Computer, disk, file-system, memory and network forensics.

* Device forensic level

    Mobile, browser, application, email and IoT forensics.

* Advanced forensic level

    Malware, software, cloud, database and wireless forensics.

* Multimedia forensic level

    Image, audio, video, GPS and drone forensics.

* Specialist forensic level

    Blockchain, vehicle, industrial, AI and identity forensics.

* Professional investigation level

    E-discovery, legal evidence, chain of custody, expert reporting and courtroom presentation.

